description:
A project dialect setting: windows (default) or mac. For mac:
- `#If Mac` is True and Win32/Win64 are False;
- POSIX path rules;
- Declare into dylibs is native on macOS and emulated or rejected elsewhere;
- MacID works;
- MacScript/AppleScriptTask is native on macOS where the sandbox allows, rejected elsewhere;
- settings go where Mac Excel keeps them;
- CreateObject fails as in Mac Excel (no COM).

The semantics come from the Mac reference (the Mac Excel reference bead). Done: the probes match the Mac answers on macOS, and on the other hosts through shims.

Owner 2026-11-23: CalcFunc as function libraries for Python, .NET and JavaScript that evaluate exactly like Excel. A spreadsheet calculation turned into a Python tool or notebook, a C# program or a JavaScript app then gets the same bits from the same functions, with bit-exactness managed by profile as in DNA Calc. Low priority.

The libraries are published from the functions handbook, as part of the bigger handbook project (dc-dfl, handbook.dnacalc.com, the ExcelFunctionsHandbook repository): each function's handbook page shows its Python, .NET and JavaScript call with its per-function status, and the packages' documentation is generated from the handbook records.

The bindings are generated from the CalcFunc catalogue metadata (calc-catalogue, plus whatever extra metadata the design finds missing, added to the catalogue itself), never written by hand per function. The engine-no-more-than-Excel rule holds: the libraries expose Excel's functions with Excel's semantics, nothing extra.

Children:
- .1 shared design (boundary, metadata, value model, hard cases, profiles);
- .2 Python;
- .3 .NET;
- .4 JavaScript;
- .5 publishing through the handbook.

Publishing to PyPI, NuGet or npm needs the owner's yes and the licence-clean state of dc-96l.

comments.text:
Owner 2026-11-23 on range vs literal: maybe a flag, maybe a separate structure, e.g. RangeWrapper(myArray); not sure. Coordinator input:
- prefer a Range type per argument over a per-call flag, because a call can mix both kinds;
- it mirrors Excel's own split between a reference and an array (xltypeRef against xltypeMulti; ExcelReference against object[,] in Excel-DNA);
- plain lists and numpy arrays behave as Excel arrays;
- Range carries Empty cells and optional sheet state (hidden rows for SUBTOTAL and AGGREGATE).
Array lifting: Excel broadcasting is not numpy's:
- mismatched shapes fill #N/A instead of raising;
- which arguments lift is per argument;
- nested array results give #CALC!;
- a range in a scalar position lifts in dynamic-array Excel but intersects with the calling cell in older Excel, so this belongs to the profile;
- lifting runs inside Rust in one call.

Owner 2026-11-23: prefers the simpler container plan:
- plain containers (lists, numpy arrays, DataFrame columns) have range semantics;
- scalars are literal;
- library results come back tagged as Excel arrays;
- a cell() or Range([[x]]) helper covers a single cell.
Design on that basis. Per-parameter lifting comes from dc-dfl.2.6.

description:
Python library over CalcFunc, per the shared design.

The package: an abi3 wheel, Windows x64 and Linux x64 first. Its module exposes Excel's worksheet functions with:
- generated signatures;
- docstrings from the functions handbook;
- .pyi type stubs;
- the per-function status.

Values:
- float, str, bool;
- an ExcelError enum;
- Empty and Missing sentinels;
- a Range type for reference semantics;
- 2-D arrays as nested lists, or as numpy arrays (optional dependency).

Vectorized entry points, so a numpy column costs one boundary crossing. Profiles per call or per session.

Include a notebook example that recomputes a real spreadsheet model, for example an amortization schedule with PMT, IPMT and PPMT, and shows the same bits as Excel.

Tests: the witness bank through Python, bit for bit. A Pyodide build comes later. Publishing to PyPI needs the owner's yes.

JavaScript and TypeScript library over CalcFunc, per the shared design.

The package: an npm package wrapping the soft-x87 wasm build of calc-func through wasm-bindgen, for Node and browsers. It has:
- generated TypeScript signatures and .d.ts files from the catalogue metadata;
- JSDoc from the functions handbook.

Values:
- number, string, boolean;
- an ExcelError class;
- Empty and Missing sentinels;
- a Range wrapper;
- 2-D arrays, with typed-array (Float64Array) entry points for numeric columns.

Notes:
- JavaScript's own Math must never be used for a function's arithmetic.
- The wasm module loads once. It is synchronous after an async init.
- The same build can later back the Office.js custom-functions face (dc-17spk.6), where that helps.

Tests: the witness bank through Node, bit for bit, plus one browser smoke. Publishing to npm needs the owner's yes.

DNA Calc's file route (an opened .xlsm whose document events run: calc-vba load_file_project_with, DocumentEvents::All) answers 5 cases of the simple-UDF and DnaExamples sets differently from Excel since master merged the CalcVBA pins 9cf7dcfc (d86c181f, engine-served Scripting.Dictionary) and 7d554eb7 (2141cb13, dc-hgie capability policy). Before them (pin e42bf5bb, with the dc-hsa5.12 changes of 2026-11-12) the same route answered 244 of 244 and DNA Calc's page 668 of 668 (crates/calc-xll-reference/traces/2026-11-11-simple-udfs/README.md, last section).

Measured on Windows at the merged tree (apps/dna-calc-worker/tests/opened_vba.rs the_simple_udf_sets_answer_as_excel_does_in_the_macro_workbooks, the error text read from calc-vba's call):
- =SimpleKind(A1) in DnaSimple.xlsm and DnaExamples.xlsm: VBA error 13 "Type mismatch" (TypeName of a Range argument; Excel "Range");
- =DnaCounterName() (TypeName(New DnaCounter), a project class): error 13 (Excel "DnaCounter");
- =DnaDictType() (TypeName of As New Scripting.Dictionary): "DnaScriptingDictionary", the engine's class name (Excel "Dictionary");
- =DnaSelection() (TypeName(Selection)): error 429 "ActiveX component can't create object" (Excel "Range").
A plain project with a class (no Office references) still answers TypeName(New Class) correctly on both backends with and without a Full policy scope, so the difference comes with the file project's references or the engine-supplied Dictionary program; OneCalc's file route (DocumentEvents::Workbook) shows 2 mismatches in dna-examples/xlsm in crates/calc-vba/tests/corpus/baseline.json, likely the same two class cases.

Fixed when: those 5 cases answer as Excel in that worker test (its known-difference list for this bead is then empty) and through DNA Calc's page (cargo xtask app xll-accept calc, sets dnasimple-xlsm, dnaexamples-xlsm-simple, dnaexamples-xlsm).

In a document project (an opened .xlsm, DNA Calc's file route with automatic document events) every worksheet function used to be bound to CalcVBA's owning typed entry (`bind_values`), and one function the typed route could not link refused the whole project. Since dc-hsa5.12 (2026-11-12) such a function runs on the dynamic runtime, as from an add-in or a module (crates/calc-vba/src/runtime/cache.rs, `OwnedSession::dynamic`), and is a typed refusal only when called inside a running document event (it cannot enter the session the event holds).

What the owning typed route does not carry today (CalcVBA pin e42bf5bb), measured on DnaSimple.bas and DnaExamples.bas:
- `TypeName` and other VBA library calls without an owning builtin (calcvba-rt-abi host_entry.rs `owning_builtin` lists IsMissing, IsArray, VarType, CStr, CVar, Left, Right, Mid, UCase, LCase, Trim, LTrim, RTrim, Replace, Len, InStr, CreateObject): "unlinked typed source project VBA (ModuleFunc { module: Information, member: TypeName })";
- `Dim x As New SomeClass` (AsNew with a project class: "requires the dynamic runtime");
- ParamArray, Date, Currency, Range (object) and array signatures, and more arguments than the typed slot bound.

Fixed when: the owning typed route links these (a CalcVBA change and pin), `OwnedSession::dynamic` is None for every function of fixture/vba/DnaSimple.bas and DnaExamples.bas, and a nested Change/Calculate event that recalculates such a function answers instead of refusing. Evidence of the current state: apps/dna-calc-worker/tests/opened_vba.rs `the_simple_udf_sets_answer_as_excel_does_in_the_macro_workbooks` (244 of 244 on the dynamic fallback).

